    <statement><p>Match the functions with the graphs of their domains.</p>
<p><ol>
<li>
<p><m>f(x,y) = \sqrt{x^{2}y^{5}}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>f(x,y) = \ln\mathopen{}\left(3x-3y\right)</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>f(x,y) = 3x-3y</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>f(x,y) = e^{\frac{1}{3x-3y}}</m></p>
</li>
</ol></p>
<p xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal"><ol label="A.">
<li>
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__WHFreeman__Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition__14_Differentiation_in_Several_Variables__14_1_Functions_of_Two_or_More_Variables__14_1_7_pg-image-1.png" width="33%"/>
</li>
<li>
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__WHFreeman__Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition__14_Differentiation_in_Several_Variables__14_1_Functions_of_Two_or_More_Variables__14_1_7_pg-image-2.png" width="33%"/>
</li>
<li>
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__WHFreeman__Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition__14_Differentiation_in_Several_Variables__14_1_Functions_of_Two_or_More_Variables__14_1_7_pg-image-3.png" width="33%"/>
</li>
<li>
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__WHFreeman__Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition__14_Differentiation_in_Several_Variables__14_1_Functions_of_Two_or_More_Variables__14_1_7_pg-image-4.png" width="33%"/>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <solution><m>\ln\mathopen{}\left(3x-3y\right)</m> is the region <m>3x-3y &gt; 0</m>, which is the half-plane with boundary <m>3x-3y = 0</m>.




The domain of <m>3x-3y</m> is the entire plane.




The domain of <m>\sqrt{x^{2}y^{5}}</m> is the region <m>x^{2}y^{5} \geq 0</m>. The domain will include the lines <m>x = 0</m> and <m>y = 0</m>. The factor <m>x^{2}</m> is positive for all positive <m>x</m> and negative for all negative <m>x</m>. The factor <m>y^{5}</m> is positive for all positive <m>y</m> and negative for all negative <m>y</m>. The product is positive if both factors are positive, or both factors are negative. This occurs in Quadrant I and Quadrant II.




The domain of <m>e^{\frac{1}{3x-3y}}</m> is the same as the domain of the exponent. This means that the domain is the same as the domain of <m>\frac{1}{3x-3y}</m>, which is the set line <m>3x-3y \neq 0</m>. Thus, the domain is entire plane except for the line <m>3x-3y = 0</m>. 


</solution>
